# Custom validators (beyond the linter)

## When you need them

ESLint/Biome/Ruff cannot express:

- File-name role conventions (`.service.ts`, `.entity.ts`)
- Folder / package placement vs the durable layout map ([`folder-structure`](./folder-structure.mdc))
- Domain construction invariants (`private constructor` + factory methods)
- Cross-package import boundaries
- Config-shaped literal patterns on new lines only
- Branch/PR naming against org policy
- Monorepo registry ↔ filesystem drift

## Pattern

```
validator CLI
 ├── parse git diff (BASE..HEAD)
 ├── classify each added/changed path or line
 ├── verdict: error (new) | warning (pre-existing)
 └── exit non-zero on blocking findings
```

**Requirements:**

- Unit-test the classifiers (regex, AST helpers, path rules)
- Support local run with same env vars as CI (`BASE_REF`, `HEAD_REF`)
- Document in a playbook, not only in CI YAML

## File role suffixes (optional but high-leverage)

Enforce a **closed allow-list** of dotted suffixes that encode architectural role:

| Suffix | Typical meaning |
| --------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `.service` | Orchestration |
| `.use-case` | Single application operation |
| `.entity` / `.value-object` | Domain with invariants |
| `.repository` / `.adapter` | I/O boundary |
| `.port` | Injected dependency interface |
| `.dto` / `.shape` | Passive data |
| `.controller` / `.handler` | HTTP/CLI entry |
| `.spec` / `.test` | Tests |

One suffix per file. Naked names only on explicit allow-list (`index`, entrypoints, migrations).

**No validator in the repo?** Review still applies this allow-list (or the repo’s documented substitute) on **new/changed** paths — see [`ci-taxonomy`](./ci-taxonomy.mdc) § Adapt when missing. Do not wait for a CI job that does not exist.

New top-level folders or layer homes that disagree with the layout blueprint are structure findings — update the map or reject the path ([`folder-structure`](./folder-structure.mdc)).

